[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"TypeScript Fundamentals in Angular"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Angular, a powerful framework for building web applications, leverages TypeScript to provide structure, maintainability, and enhanced developer experience. This tutorial covers fundamental TypeScript concepts essential for Angular development: Types, Interfaces, Classes, Decorators, and Generics."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Types"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript is a statically typed language, meaning that the type of a variable is known at compile time. This allows the compiler to catch type-related errors early on, improving code quality. Common types include:"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`number`: Represents numeric values (e.g., 10, 3.14)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`string`: Represents textual data (e.g., "Hello, world!")."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`boolean`: Represents true or false values."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`any`: Represents any type (use sparingly, as it defeats the purpose of TypeScript's type checking)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`void`: Represents the absence of a value, typically used for functions that don't return anything."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`null` and `undefined`: Represent the absence of a value in different ways."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Type annotations
let age: number = 30;
let name: string = "John Doe";
let isStudent: boolean = false;"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Interfaces"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interfaces define contracts that specify the properties and methods an object should have. They are a powerful way to enforce structure and consistency in your code."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface Person {
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  age: number;
  greet(): string;
}

let person: Person = {
  firstName: "Jane",
  lastName: "Doe",
  age: 25,
  greet() {
    return `Hello, my name is ${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`;
  }
};"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Classes"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Classes are blueprints for creating objects. They encapsulate data (properties) and behavior (methods). Angular heavily relies on classes for defining components, services, and other building blocks."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
  name: string;

  constructor(name: string) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  makeSound() {
    console.log("Generic animal sound");
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  breed: string;

  constructor(name: string, breed: string) {
    super(name);
    this.breed = breed;
  }

  makeSound() {
    console.log("Woof!");
  }
}

let animal = new Animal("Generic Animal");
animal.makeSound(); // Output: Generic animal sound

let dog = new Dog("Buddy", "Golden Retriever");
dog.makeSound(); // Output: Woof!"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Decorators"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Decorators are a special kind of declaration that can be attached to classes, methods, properties, or parameters. They provide a way to add metadata and modify the behavior of the decorated element. Angular uses decorators extensively, such as `@Component`, `@Injectable`, and `@Input`."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"function logClass(constructor: Function) {
  console.log("Class decorated: ", constructor.name);
}

@logClass
class MyComponent {
  constructor() {
    console.log("MyComponent initialized");
  }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Angular, the `@Component` decorator is crucial for defining components:"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-component',
  templateUrl: './my-component.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./my-component.component.css']
})
export class MyComponent {
  // Component logic here
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Generics"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics allow you to write reusable code that can work with different types without sacrificing type safety. They provide a way to parameterize types, making your code more flexible and maintainable."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"function identity<T>(arg: T): T {
  return arg;
}

let myString: string = identity<string>("hello");
let myNumber: number = identity<number>(123);"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Angular, generics are often used in services to handle different types of data:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class DataService {

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) { }

  getData<T>(url: string): Observable<T> {
    return this.http.get<T>(url);
  }
}"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding TypeScript fundamentals is crucial for effective Angular development. By mastering types, interfaces, classes, decorators, and generics, you can write cleaner, more maintainable, and robust Angular applications."}]
